  "full_text": " That seems too simple. Is there another way to think about this problem?\n\nWait, maybe I should consider different units or think about it in another way. Hmm, but it's a classic problem, isn't it? Maybe the confusion arises from the wording? Let me try another approach.\n\nSuppose the ball costs \\( x \\) cents. Then, the bat costs \\( 1.10 - x \\) dollars. Wait, but the bat costs one dollar more than the ball. So, in dollars, \\( \\text{Bat} = \\text{Ball} + 1.00 \\). Maybe I need to set up equations in dollars instead of cents.\n\nLet me try that. Let the ball cost \\( y \\) dollars. Then, the bat costs \\( y + 1.00 \\) dollars. Together, they add up to \\$1.10.\n\nSo, \\( y + (y + 1.00) = 1.10 \\). That simplifies to \\( 2y + 1.00 = 1.10 \\), so \\( 2y = 0.10 \\), which means \\( y = 0.05 \\)"
}